1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,109
A first-level model may contain several
explanatory variables, but a scientific

2
00:00:04,109 --> 00:00:06,351
result begins with a focused question.

3
00:00:06,351 --> 00:00:10,460
Was the response during left-button trials
greater than the response during

4
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:11,207
right-button trials?

5
00:00:11,207 --> 00:00:14,196
A contrast turns that comparison into
precise weights.

6
00:00:14,196 --> 00:00:19,426
Those weights must agree with the model
columns and with the words used to

7
00:00:19,426 --> 00:00:20,546
describe the hypothesis.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:29,883
At each voxel, FEAT fits the general
linear model.

9
00:00:29,883 --> 00:00:34,140
The design matrix contains explanatory
variables, and beta contains one fitted

10
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:36,075
coefficient for each model column.

11
00:00:36,075 --> 00:00:38,784
A contrast does not change those
estimates.

12
00:00:38,784 --> 00:00:44,589
It defines a linear combination, written
as c transpose beta, where c is the vector

13
00:00:44,589 --> 00:00:45,750
of hypothesis weights.

14
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:54,469
Suppose the explanatory-variable order is
Left followed by Right.

15
00:00:54,469 --> 00:00:57,582
The weights one, zero select the Left
coefficient.

16
00:00:57,582 --> 00:00:59,917
Zero, one select the Right coefficient.

17
00:00:59,917 --> 00:01:04,976
In a usual task model, each is tested
relative to the implicit baseline

18
00:01:04,976 --> 00:01:07,701
represented by the rest of the model.

19
00:01:07,701 --> 00:01:10,036
Neither is a direct Left-versus-Right
comparison.

20
00:01:10,036 --> 00:01:15,484
One, one tests their positive sum, while
one half, one half tests the numerical

21
00:01:15,484 --> 00:01:15,873
average.

22
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,500
To compare Left directly with Right, use
one, negative one.

23
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:31,310
This computes beta Left minus beta Right
and tests the positive direction, Left

24
00:01:31,310 --> 00:01:32,420
greater than Right.

25
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:35,379
Reversing the signs tests Right greater
than Left.

26
00:01:35,379 --> 00:01:39,819
A negative weight means that a coefficient
is subtracted in this hypothesis.

27
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:42,409
It does not, by itself, mean deactivation.

28
00:01:48,767 --> 00:01:52,440
Contrast numbers only have meaning when
aligned with the correct design-matrix

29
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:52,774
columns.

30
00:01:52,774 --> 00:01:57,784
If the actual order is Right then Left,
one, negative one tests Right greater than

31
00:01:57,784 --> 00:02:01,458
Left, even if the contrast is named Left
greater than Right.

32
00:02:01,458 --> 00:02:06,802
Write the EV order, read each contrast as
a sentence, and point from every weight to

33
00:02:06,802 --> 00:02:08,805
its displayed column before running FEAT.

34
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:18,643
In FEAT, open Full model setup, then
Contrasts and F-tests.

35
00:02:18,643 --> 00:02:23,466
Give each contrast a scientific name,
enter weights in the displayed EV order,

36
00:02:23,466 --> 00:02:26,435
and inspect the arrows in the design
preview.

37
00:02:26,435 --> 00:02:31,629
Before selecting Done, compare every row
with the analysis plan and save the design

38
00:02:31,629 --> 00:02:34,226
file so the model specification remains
reproducible.

39
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:43,009
For each contrast, FEAT writes several
related outputs.

40
00:02:43,009 --> 00:02:46,729
The cope is the weighted contrast estimate
in model units.

41
00:02:46,729 --> 00:02:49,333
The varcope quantifies uncertainty in that
estimate.

42
00:02:49,333 --> 00:02:55,284
A t statistic compares the cope with its
standard error, and FEAT converts it to a

43
00:02:55,284 --> 00:02:56,772
z statistic for inference.

44
00:02:56,772 --> 00:03:00,492
A z-statistic map is standardized
evidence, not an effect-size map.

45
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:11,992
Before running FEAT, list the EVs in
matrix order and state the scientific

46
00:03:11,992 --> 00:03:13,580
question for every contrast.

47
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:18,342
Translate the sentence into weights, then
translate the weights back into a

48
00:03:18,342 --> 00:03:18,738
sentence.

49
00:03:18,738 --> 00:03:21,516
Inspect the design matrix and contrast
arrows.

50
00:03:21,516 --> 00:03:25,485
Finally, distinguish the effect estimate,
its uncertainty, and the standardized

51
00:03:25,485 --> 00:03:25,881
statistic.

52
00:03:25,881 --> 00:03:31,040
The model is ready when the design,
weights, and scientific language tell the

53
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:31,834
same story.
